@@ -203,3 +203,29 @@ solver:
   absolute tolerance: 1.0e-06
 ```
 
+### M-orthonormal bases
+
+An OpInf or RBF kernel ROM whose basis satisfies
+`Phi_free.T @ M_free_free @ Phi_free = I` can opt into mass-weighted
+full-to-reduced projection:
+
+```yaml
+model:
+  type: linear opinf rom
+  model-file: opinf-operator.npz
+  basis inner product: mass
+```
+
+Norma assembles the consistent finite-element mass from the ROM's shadow FOM
+and projects displacement, velocity, and acceleration as
+`q = Phi_free.T @ M_free_free @ x_free`. Fixed-DOF rows of the basis should
+be zero; prescribed motion continues to enter through the existing boundary
+operators. Norma checks the reduced Gram matrix against the identity with an
+infinity-norm tolerance of `1e-8` and aborts if the basis and runtime mass do
+not agree.
+
+The basis and mass use component-fast nodal ordering:
+`[x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2, ...]`. Reconstruction remains `x = Phi @ q`,
+and the reduced dynamics continue to use an identity reduced mass. Omit the
+key (or set it to `euclidean`) for existing Euclidean-orthonormal models.
+
